# Machine-local and runtime state. Everything here is regenerated by dx.

# Secrets and per-machine settings.
/.env
/.env.local

# ...but the demo apps' .env files are part of the demo: they hold a committed
# throwaway APP_KEY so `dx up` works on a fresh clone, and there is nothing
# secret in them. A bare `.env` pattern would have silently excluded them, and
# the symptom is a 500 on every request in one runtime only.
!demo-apps/*/.env

# The resolved-configuration cache, rebuilt from the database on demand.
.stack.env

# The config database itself. NOT committed: it is a build artifact of
# config/*.yml plus whatever `dx config set` has done on this machine, it is
# binary and undiffable, and two developers committing it would conflict on
# every change. The seeds in config/*.yml are the committed source of truth -
# `dx config export` shows anything set at runtime that is not yet in them.
config/dx.db
config/dx.db-wal
config/dx.db-shm

# All runtime state: database files, the local CA, caches, container profiles,
# agent home directories. Never commit any of it - data/proxy alone contains a
# private key, and data/agent/ contains whatever an agent downloaded.
data/

# Snapshots are the exception people ask about: they live under data/ and are
# deliberately gitignored too. They are database dumps of a dev environment,
# which is exactly the kind of thing that turns out to contain a real customer
# record six months later.

# Scaffolding venv, created on first `dx init`.
scaffold/.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local compose overrides.
docker-compose.override.yml

# Test scratch. Sandboxes live in $TMPDIR and clean themselves up; this catches
# the case where a test was interrupted before its trap ran.
tests/**/.stack.env
tests/**/dx.db*
